\chapter{Control Plane Structure}

A cluster node is the largest unit of hierarchy: one machine's worth of
sockets, cores, memory, and devices. That machine is partitioned along
NUMA boundaries into independent kernel instances that coordinate by
message passing — a Barrelfish-style multikernel, but with the instance
resized from the core to the NUMA node. Each instance is a microkernel
whose trusted core is a framekernel: a single main-kernel address space
written in the interrupt, continuation-passing style. The framekernel
boundary sits deliberately between Asterinas and seL4/Mach: it absorbs
more of the server set than a classic microkernel would, but — unlike
strict Asterinas — it does not pull every microkernel server into the
main kernel address space, so the remaining servers keep their own
address spaces. The boundary is drawn by programming discipline rather
than by an absolute no-blocking rule: code inside the framekernel runs
as interrupt-driven state machines, while work that crosses into a
userspace server switches to the task model and enters through a
FlexSC/io\_uring-style completion interface.

